{
 "thread": "QnttkCYsqm4",
 "subject": "AR(1) Modeling",
 "messages_listed": 4,
 "last_activity": "2016-12-15T21:04:45Z",
 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "LNMxQAn3CwAJ",
   "from": "Vishv Jeet",
   "author": "fbff4100433066b4",
   "date": "2016-12-15T16:32:56Z",
   "subject": "AR(1) Modeling",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br></div><div>Consider the following simple AR(1) model;</div><div><br></div><div>data {</div><div>  int T;</div><div>  ...</div><div>}</div>parameters {<div>  vector eps[T];</div><div>  real&lt;lower=0&gt; phi;</div><div>  real&lt;lower=0&gt; sigma;</div><div>}</div><div>model {</div><div>  eps[1] ~ normal(0, sigma);</div><div>  tail(eps, T-1) ~ normal(<b>phi * head(</b>eps<b>, T-1)</b>, sigma); // AR(1) modeling suggested in STAN reference</div><div>  ....</div><div>}</div><div><br></div><div>My question is: <b>phi * head(eps, T-1) </b>is a nonlinear function of two parameters, so even though I am not directly sampling it but shouldn&#39;t this need a Jacobian adjustment because <b>eps </b>is not data but a parameter itself.</div><div><br></div><div>regards,</div><div>vishy</div><div><br></div><div><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "OpoAF5z-CwAJ",
   "from": "Ben Goodrich",
   "author": "743bbacc59f4b25f",
   "date": "2016-12-15T18:51:43Z",
   "subject": "Re: AR(1) Modeling",
   "parts": [
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">On Thursday, December 15, 2016 at 11:32:56 AM UTC-5, Vishv Jeet wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br></div><div>Consider the following simple AR(1) model;</div><div><br></div><div>data {</div><div>  int T;</div><div>  ...</div><div>}</div>parameters {<div>  vector eps[T];</div><div>  real&lt;lower=0&gt; phi;</div><div>  real&lt;lower=0&gt; sigma;</div><div>}</div><div>model {</div><div>  eps[1] ~ normal(0, sigma);</div><div>  tail(eps, T-1) ~ normal(<b>phi * head(</b>eps<b>, T-1)</b>, sigma); // AR(1) modeling suggested in STAN reference</div><div>  ....</div><div>}</div><div><br></div><div>My question is: <b>phi * head(eps, T-1) </b>is a nonlinear function of two parameters, so even though I am not directly sampling it but shouldn&#39;t this need a Jacobian adjustment because <b>eps </b>is not data but a parameter itself.</div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br>That is technically correct about the Jacobian but that is not generally how we would suggest to model an AR(1) process for _parameters_ (as opposed to data). It is usually better to do<br><br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px\"><code><div></div></code></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">data </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><div><span style=\"color:#000\">  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">int</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> T</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span></div><div><span style=\"color:#000\">  </span><span style=\"color:#660\">...</span></div><div><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span></div><span style=\"color:#000\">parameters </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span></div></code></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px\"><code><div><div><span style=\"color:#000\">   real eps_0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  vector</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">T </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> eps_raw</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></div></code></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px\"><code><div><div><span style=\"color:#000\">  real</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\">lower</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> phi</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span></div><div><span style=\"color:#000\">  real</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\">lower</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sigma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span></div><div><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span></div></div></code></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px\"><code><div><div><span style=\"color:#000\">model </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  vector</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">T</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> eps</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  eps</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> eps_0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">for</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">t </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\">T</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> <br>    eps</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">t</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> phi </span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> eps</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">t </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sigma </span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> eps_raw</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">t </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">];</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br>  target </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> normal_lpdf</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">eps_0 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">|</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sigma </span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sqrt</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> square</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">phi</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)));</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  target </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> normal_lpdf</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">eps_raw </span><span style=\"color:#660\">|</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#660\">...</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></div></code></div><br>In this parameterization, the primitive parameters eps_raw are iid standard normal a priori, which makes it much easier for Stan to move around than if eps were a primitive parameter. The reason to treat eps_0 separately is that sigma is the standard deviation of the errors _conditional on on eps[t - 1]_. The marginal standard deviation is deflated by sqrt(1 - square(phi)).<br><br>Ben<br><br><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
