  {
   "id": "UEnhhN4FDAAJ",
   "from": "Ben Goodrich",
   "author": "743bbacc59f4b25f",
   "date": "2016-12-15T21:04:45Z",
   "subject": "Re: AR(1) Modeling",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">On Thursday, December 15, 2016 at 2:47:01 PM UTC-5, Vishv Jeet wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Thank you Ben. This is very helpful. Just curious, was the way I had written the model wrong or computationally inefficient?</div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br>It was only wrong in the prior for the initial period was inconsistent with the generative model for the remaining periods. But it presumably would have had very low effective sample sizes for the reason I gave<br> </div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>In this parameterization, the primitive parameters eps_raw are iid standard normal a priori, which makes it much easier for Stan to move around than if eps were a primitive parameter.<br></div></div></blockquote></div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br>For large values of phi, it would be very difficult for the individual elements of eps to move freely throughout the posterior distribution, which does not occur if eps_raw is the primitive parameter.<br><br>Ben <br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  }
 ]
